  "selection": "Entire urban data-transmission-frequency experiment (v30 directory label). Included all 20 per-run text files in 3 interval settings by 2 network labels. Six corresponding merged files were compared only, with all three timing columns identical in row order to the concatenated runs. No included run windows overlap. Standard 50 ms runs and other scenarios are outside this subset. Published totals >150,000 refer to the overall dataset. The readme and 26 timing files were verified independently against the archive and the immutable Git blob IDs.",
  "measurement": "Real vehicle-to-network-to-vehicle MQTT echo. Both pub_time (publish) and sub_time (vehicle receives returned echo) are timestamped on the same onboard device in integer milliseconds. Across 106,582 complete rows, sub_time minus pub_time equals delay exactly. Millisecond storage does not establish timestamp accuracy. The dataset distinguishes the private network n8 and public network n78. Paired sign or count alone does not establish independence, physical route alignment or causality.",
  "limits": "Cloud-side receipt/forwarding, sensor generation, application control decision and actuator use times are absent from this logging schema. Echo RTT is not one-way latency and is not the duration of a full control loop. Only completed cycles are visible; the text has no independent counter of attempted transmissions, so loss cannot be measured here from timestamp gaps. Timing spans are per run and include long pauses without imputing their cause. No clock comparison between cloud and vehicle is possible from these three time fields.",
  "calculation": "Per-run median and nearest-rank p95 RTT include all completed rows. The adjacent pairs column counts cases where the next recorded publish precedes the preceding echo arrival (in the common integer-ms clock). The echo-age proxy treats publish time as creation and return of its echo as the update at that vehicle. For each nondecreasing arrival stream, integrate the elapsed time since publication of the most recently returned echo from first through last reception: sum_i [delay_i * (r_{i+1}-r_i) + (r_{i+1}-r_i)^2/2]. The integral is stored doubled as an integer, and divided by twice the time span for the mean. Zero-length intervals from timestamp ties contribute zero. No prior or post-run time and no hypothesized holds at controller application included.",
  "primary_paper": {
    "title": "5G communication delay dataset for cloud-based vehicle planning and control",
